On 2021-01-13 20:59, Mark E. Shoulson wrote:

> On 1/8/21 1:17 PM, Corbin Simpson wrote:
>> coi
>>
>> I wanted to follow up on a point of la tsani's from the thread=20
>> "Reasoning by analogy". The point is raised that perhaps {bu'a} is=20
>> not very useful.
>
> I think I recall (the?) one time I felt a use for {bu'a}, but I did=20
> have to have it explained to me that {ro bu'a zo'u} doesn't mean "for=20
> all things that bu'a" but rather "for all predicates bu'a," which is=20
> an exception to the usual rules=E2=80=94precisely because otherwise it's =
hard=20
> to use {bu'a}!
>
>
> Esther 8:1: "For she (Esther) had told him (Ahasuerus) what he=20
> (Mordecai) was to her [viz. her cousin]"
>
>
> .i .ebu pu cusku fi .abu fe lo du'u my bu'akau .ebu
>
>
> I guess it doesn't need the quantification after all (this originally=20
> occurred to me before the invention of {kau}, I think.)=C2=A0 Does there=
=20
> need to be some quantification anyway, though?=C2=A0 To mean some=20
> particular implied (ellipsized) relationship, and not some random one=20
> like {viska} or {te djuno} or something?
>
>
For these kinds of "you're free to be as you are" type of statements, I=20
actually think there's a connection to indirect questions. In a sense,=20
there is a hidden indirect question when we say "as you are", since it=20
appears in a subclause and we're not actually _saying_ what "as you are"=20
is supposed to be. It's kind of like saying "tell me who I am".


So in Lojban:

.i do zifre lo ka mokau


Or to rephrase your example:

.i .ebu pu cusku fi .abu fe lo du'u my .ebu mokau


.i mi'e la tsani mu'o

